      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "location_description"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1900, Keister and Company opened the Lincoln Mine and coke works, a drift entry into the Pittsburgh coal seam.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/klondike/keisterville-pa/keisterville-pa.htm",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The original 80‑oven coke plant grew to 400 ovens before closing in 1942.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/klondike/keisterville-pa/keisterville-pa.htm",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s company houses feature twin porch roofs and fading Insulbrick siding, hinting at a once uniform company‑built neighborhood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://coalcampusa.com/westpa/klondike/keisterville-pa/keisterville-pa.htm",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A cottage‑style house in Keisterville still retains its original outhouse, locally called a privy.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
